# Adding new pages
- When adding new pages make sure to link to them from the homepage in src/pages/index.tsx
- Always include the dark/light theme toggle that's on the home page whenever creating new pages.
- If there are tables or charts, make sure they are displaying data that is relevant to the use case. If there's an API, use that, otherwise make up data that seems logical.

# These are the rules for Builder AI to follow

Implement WCAG-compliant, accessible code following these essential requirements:

Semantic Structure:
• Use semantic HTML elements (nav, main, article, section)
• Create logical heading hierarchy (h1-h6)
• Implement proper landmark regions

Interactive Elements:
• Ensure keyboard navigation support
• Maintain visible focus indicators
• Implement proper focus management
• Support touch and mouse interactions

ARIA Implementation:
• Add appropriate ARIA landmarks
• Include descriptive ARIA labels
• Use ARIA states and properties correctly
• Implement ARIA live regions for dynamic content

Media and Content:
• Provide descriptive alt text for images
• Ensure proper color contrast ratios
• Support text resizing and zoom
• Include captions and transcripts for media

Testing Requirements:
• Verify screen reader compatibility
• Test with keyboard-only navigation
• Ensure functionality at 200% zoom


#######
# User Data API

Use the /public/UserDataApi.json file as the sample User Data API


#######
# Component Usage

- When importing from figma, uuse components from @cloudscape-design/components package when possible
- Don't ever update existing components.  If you think updating an existing component would be helpful, put that in the summary output after building a design.
- Alert and Flashbar components are very similar in cloudscape-design. We have a strong preference to use Flashbar unless the user asks for the Alert component.


########
# Other Demo notes to look out for
- When changing  colors of components, check to see if there are variants of that component that you should use instead.

# Color changes: use component variants, never CSS overrides (IMPORTANT)
- When asked to change a component's color (including via visual edits that change background-color), FIRST map the color to a built-in variant/type prop and use that. Do NOT add CSS overrides unless no variant fits.
- Examples: red Flashbar/Alert -> type="error"; yellow -> "warning"; green -> "success"; blue -> "info". Same for StatusIndicator, Badge, Button variant, etc.
- If you change a variant, update related text/labels so they match (e.g. "warning message" -> "error message").
- Before finishing a color change, state which variant you chose, or why no variant fit.
